WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.753
Our helper has a new morning job.

2
00:00:04.753 --> 00:00:08.921
Phone the supplier, and ask which of yesterday's orders went wrong.

3
00:00:09.133 --> 00:00:11.208
Most days, that takes a minute.

4
00:00:11.208 --> 00:00:13.349
Question out, answer back, done.

5
00:00:13.967 --> 00:00:18.133
One morning, the supplier picks up, says nothing, and never hangs up.

6
00:00:18.333 --> 00:00:21.578
The helper was told to call and wait for the answer.

7
00:00:21.578 --> 00:00:26.195
So it stands there with the phone at its ear, until you come back at noon.

8
00:00:26.195 --> 00:00:26.819
No report.

9
00:00:26.819 --> 00:00:28.067
And no error either.

10
00:00:28.267 --> 00:00:31.556
A call that never ends does not look like a failure.

11
00:00:31.556 --> 00:00:32.757
It just looks slow.

12
00:00:32.757 --> 00:00:34.465
So two questions for today.

13
00:00:34.465 --> 00:00:36.363
How long should a script wait?

14
00:00:36.363 --> 00:00:39.841
And how do you test the bad days, without the internet?

15
00:00:40.567 --> 00:00:47.737
In computer terms, the supplier is a web service such as GitHub, where code and its automatic checks live.

16
00:00:47.737 --> 00:00:50.713
It opens a door for programs, called an API.

17
00:00:50.933 --> 00:00:54.673
The helper's question is a request, sent to a web address.

18
00:00:54.900 --> 00:01:04.080
The answer comes back with a status code, like 200 for fine, or 404 for not found, and a body, often JSON text.

19
00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:08.611
In Python, the requests library makes that call in one line.

20
00:01:08.833 --> 00:01:10.349
Now the silent morning.

21
00:01:10.349 --> 00:01:14.172
The server accepts the connection, and then never answers.

22
00:01:14.172 --> 00:01:16.083
How long does this line wait?

23
00:01:16.083 --> 00:01:17.137
Pause and guess.

24
00:01:19.367 --> 00:01:19.949
Forever.

25
00:01:19.949 --> 00:01:22.279
requests has no default timeout.

26
00:01:22.279 --> 00:01:25.701
As long as the connection stays open, it waits.

27
00:01:25.933 --> 00:01:30.953
It is the most patient library you will ever meet, and that is exactly the problem.

28
00:01:31.167 --> 00:01:33.106
So we always pass a timeout.

29
00:01:33.106 --> 00:01:40.311
This one waits up to 3.05 seconds to connect, then up to 10 seconds of silence while the answer arrives.

30
00:01:40.533 --> 00:01:43.993
After that, the helper hangs up and reports an error.

31
00:01:43.993 --> 00:01:45.560
Loud, early, and honest.

32
00:01:45.767 --> 00:01:46.964
Here is the rule.

33
00:01:46.964 --> 00:01:50.767
A call without a timeout is a promise to wait forever.

34
00:01:50.767 --> 00:01:51.824
Always set one.

35
00:01:52.633 --> 00:01:54.148
Next, the bad answers.

36
00:01:54.148 --> 00:01:58.417
You might think: easy, if anything goes wrong, just call back.

37
00:01:58.417 --> 00:02:00.483
Retry everything, three times.

38
00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:03.483
Let's be fair and try it on three bad days.

39
00:02:03.483 --> 00:02:04.584
The line is busy.

40
00:02:04.584 --> 00:02:06.655
The supplier says no such order.

41
00:02:06.655 --> 00:02:10.668
Or your key card, the token that proves who you are, is wrong.

42
00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:11.625
Busy line?

43
00:02:11.625 --> 00:02:14.161
Calling back later can really help.

44
00:02:14.161 --> 00:02:16.189
Next time, someone picks up.

45
00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:17.397
No such order?

46
00:02:17.397 --> 00:02:20.532
Call back, and you hear no such order again.

47
00:02:20.532 --> 00:02:21.244
And again.

48
00:02:21.244 --> 00:02:23.168
The answer will not change.

49
00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:24.922
Same for a bad key card.

50
00:02:24.922 --> 00:02:28.917
The supplier will not suddenly like your card on the third try.

51
00:02:29.133 --> 00:02:34.570
So the lab retries only when asking again can help.

52
00:02:34.570 --> 00:02:37.022
429, too many requests.

53
00:02:37.022 --> 00:02:39.474
A 500, 502, 503 or 504.

54
00:02:39.474 --> 00:02:42.032
Or a dropped connection.

55
00:02:42.032 --> 00:02:45.124
Never a 401, and never a 404.

56
00:02:45.333 --> 00:02:48.122
And only for GET, the method for reading.

57
00:02:48.122 --> 00:02:49.958
A repeated GET is harmless.

58
00:02:49.958 --> 00:02:52.543
A repeated POST may start two deploys.

59
00:02:53.267 --> 00:02:59.217
If the line stays busy, the helper waits a little longer each time, so the server can recover.

60
00:02:59.217 --> 00:03:03.648
With a backoff factor of 1, that is zero, then two, then four seconds.

61
00:03:04.367 --> 00:03:05.344
So far so good.

62
00:03:05.344 --> 00:03:07.560
But how do you test the busy line?

63
00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:12.643
You cannot ask GitHub to be busy on Tuesday at ten so the helper can practise.

64
00:03:12.867 --> 00:03:21.108
And tests that call the real service are slow, they fail now and then for reasons outside your code, and they need secrets in CI.

65
00:03:21.733 --> 00:03:23.728
So, a friend plays the supplier.

66
00:03:23.728 --> 00:03:27.592
The friend answers the phone and says whatever you wrote down.

67
00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:29.491
Notice what did not change.

68
00:03:29.491 --> 00:03:32.121
The helper and its checklist are the same.

69
00:03:32.121 --> 00:03:34.625
Only the far end of the line is pretend.

70
00:03:34.833 --> 00:03:38.202
In Python, that friend is a library called responses.

71
00:03:38.202 --> 00:03:42.079
It steps in exactly where requests hands data to the network.

72
00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:45.702
The test writes the answers down in order.

73
00:03:45.702 --> 00:03:47.647
A 502 first, then a 200.

74
00:03:47.867 --> 00:03:50.761
Now we run the real code against the friend.

75
00:03:50.761 --> 00:03:52.274
What will the test see?

76
00:03:52.274 --> 00:03:53.327
Pause and guess.

77
00:03:55.533 --> 00:03:56.902
First call: 502.

78
00:03:56.902 --> 00:04:00.153
The line is busy, so Retry calls back.

79
00:04:00.153 --> 00:04:01.608
Second call: 200.

80
00:04:01.608 --> 00:04:05.715
The run passes, and that proves the retry works.

81
00:04:05.933 --> 00:04:10.868
The test passes backoff=0, so it does not even sleep between the calls.

82
00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:16.524
And if the helper dials a number you never wrote down, the friend raises ConnectionError.

83
00:04:16.524 --> 00:04:19.753
So a test cannot reach the real internet by accident.

84
00:04:20.367 --> 00:04:22.488
A stand-in like this has a name.

85
00:04:22.488 --> 00:04:23.813
It is called a mock.

86
00:04:23.813 --> 00:04:30.243
The lab's eight GitHub tests use one, and they run in about a tenth of a second, with no network.

87
00:04:30.867 --> 00:04:33.232
Amazon's cloud gets a friend too.

88
00:04:33.232 --> 00:04:44.555
Inside a test, moto catches each request just before it would be sent, and answers from a fake AWS that lives only in memory, and starts empty for every test.

89
00:04:44.767 --> 00:04:46.852
But a friend is not the supplier.

90
00:04:46.852 --> 00:04:52.222
moto does not check permissions, the rules for who may do what, unless you ask it to.

91
00:04:52.222 --> 00:04:56.392
So a test can pass while the real account would say access denied.

92
00:04:56.600 --> 00:04:58.231
So here is the guardrail.

93
00:04:58.231 --> 00:05:01.298
A fake proves your logic, not your permissions.

94
00:05:01.298 --> 00:05:05.473
Run a new check once against a real account before you trust it.

95
00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:07.843
One more rule from the moto docs.

96
00:05:07.843 --> 00:05:10.700
Start the mock before you create any client.

97
00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:13.037
A client made earlier is not mocked.

98
00:05:13.767 --> 00:05:15.605
So, the helper's new habits.

99
00:05:15.605 --> 00:05:16.655
Hang up in time.

100
00:05:16.655 --> 00:05:18.756
Call back only when it can help.

101
00:05:18.756 --> 00:05:22.497
And rehearse with a friend, then try the real thing once.

102
00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:25.108
Quick check before you go.

103
00:05:25.108 --> 00:05:26.652
The answer is a 401.

104
00:05:26.652 --> 00:05:28.197
Your token is wrong.

105
00:05:28.197 --> 00:05:30.359
Should the helper call back?

106
00:05:32.567 --> 00:05:32.819
No.

107
00:05:32.819 --> 00:05:35.089
The answer will not change.

108
00:05:35.089 --> 00:05:36.939
Fix the token instead.
